MiiPU: A Categorical Cognitive Processor for the Mii Logical Architecture

Technical Specification Paper — Version 1.0

**Ouadi Maakoul**
Independent Research · May 2026

---

## Abstract

We present the **Mii Processing Unit (MiiPU)**, a purpose-built heterogeneous chiplet processor whose instruction set, memory architecture, and functional units are derived directly from the categorical structure of the Mii Logical Architecture — a tripartite cognitive framework formalizing the interaction between conscious reasoning (CR), subconscious pattern processing (SP), and meta-cognitive coupling (Mii) via two fundamental adjunctions: Formalisation ⊣ Grounding and Pattern Lifting ⊣ Quotient. We demonstrate that standard GPU architectures are structurally mismatched to this workload, suffering up to 80% silicon underutilization on the T-functor iteration cycle T(C,S) = (G(P(S)), Q(F(C))). The MiiPU resolves this by mapping every mathematical primitive — translation-table lookups, Jaccard coherence computation, sheaf gluing, and Kleene fixed-point iteration — to a dedicated hardware unit. The three-chiplet design (CR-Tile, SP-Tile, Mii-Tile) is integrated on a TSMC CoWoS silicon interposer with UCIe 3.0 die-to-die links at 64 GT/s and four stacks of HBM4 delivering 8 TB/s aggregate bandwidth. Projected performance on one complete T-functor cycle is **3.5 µs**, a 24× improvement over an A100 GPU baseline. The MiiPU constitutes the first processor in which the adjunction F ⊣ G is a **native hardware instruction**, not a software approximation.

