## 1. Introduction

The history of processor design has followed workload. Floating-point units emerged for scientific computing; SIMD units for signal processing; tensor cores for deep learning. Each time a new computational paradigm was identified, silicon eventually followed. The Mii Logical Architecture represents such a paradigm: a formal, category-theoretic model of cognitive dynamics whose computational primitives — adjunction evaluation, translation-table matching, sheaf gluing, and Scott-continuous fixed-point iteration — belong to none of the classes that existing processors efficiently execute.

The problem is not one of raw performance. It is one of **architectural mismatch**. A GPU is an array of floating-point multipliers arranged to execute dense matrix operations in lockstep. The Mii workload is, at its heart, a bidirectional associative lookup (the translation table T = {(c, s, w)}) fed into a convergence loop over a complete partial order. These are fundamentally different computational shapes. Throwing more CUDA cores at the problem does not solve it — it accelerates the wrong operation.

This paper makes the following contributions:

1. A formal mapping from every mathematical operation in the Mii Logical Architecture to a hardware functional unit.
2. A three-chiplet processor architecture (CR-Tile, SP-Tile, Mii-Tile) whose silicon area is entirely devoted to Mii-native operations.
3. A native instruction set (MiiISA) exposing the two fundamental adjunctions F ⊣ G and P ⊣ Q as first-class hardware instructions.
4. Package integration specifications using current production technology: UCIe 3.0 (released August 2025, 64 GT/s), HBM4 (JEDEC JESD270-4, April 2025, 2 TB/s per stack), and TSMC CoWoS Gen 6 (2026, N3 process node).
5. A three-phase implementation roadmap from FPGA prototype to production ASIC.

