## 2. Background and Motivation

### 2.1 Why Existing Hardware Fails

Research on neurosymbolic hardware has converged on a consistent diagnosis. The REASON framework (2026) identifies that probabilistic logical reasoning — the closest prior art to CR-layer computation — suffers from **irregular control flow, low arithmetic intensity, uncoalesced memory accesses, and poor hardware utilization** on both CPUs and GPUs. CogSys (2025) demonstrates that executing neurosymbolic workloads on current CPU/GPU/TPU architectures leads to **severe hardware underutilization** due to higher memory intensity, greater compute heterogeneity, and access pattern irregularity. NSFlow (2025, IBM Research, DAC) confirms that current ASIC accelerators designed for neural workloads are **incompatible** with the heterogeneous kernel types that symbolic-neural systems require.

The Mii architecture compounds these difficulties. It introduces four operations that no existing accelerator handles natively:

| Operation | Root Cause of Inefficiency | GPU Overhead |
|---|---|---|
| Translation-table lookup T = (c, s, w) | Random associative access vs. strided GPU cache | >400 ns per query |
| Coherence factor coh(f,g) = \|A∩B\|/\|A∪B\| | Serial set-intersection on CUDA ALUs | 20–30 cycle penalty |
| Sheaf gluing M(U) → ∏ M(Uᵢ) | Cross-kernel synchronization breaking warp coherence | ~25 µs per merge |
| Kleene chain T^n(⊥) convergence | Kernel launch overhead ~5 µs per iteration × thousands of iterations | ~85 µs per T-cycle |

### 2.2 The Neurosymbolic Hardware Landscape

The closest existing work is the CogSys architecture, which proposes reconfigurable neuro/symbolic processing elements (nsPEs) with bubble-streaming dataflow, achieving **>75× speedup over TPU-like systolic arrays** at TSMC 28 nm with under 5% area overhead. NSFlow provides an end-to-end FPGA framework for neurosymbolic AI with adaptive systolic arrays and reconfigurable memory. The REASON accelerator targets probabilistic logical reasoning with a tree-based processing fabric achieving 12–50× speedup over desktop GPUs.

None of these systems contains: (a) a hardware adjunction evaluator implementing the natural bijection Φ_{C,M}: Hom(F(C), M) → Hom(C, G(M)); (b) a sheaf coprocessor implementing the equalizer condition of Theorem 6.3.1; or (c) a Kleene convergence controller with formal fixed-point guarantees. The MiiPU fills this gap.

