## 8. Package Integration

### 8.1 Silicon Interposer (TSMC CoWoS Gen 6)

The MiiPU package is assembled on a TSMC CoWoS (Chip-on-Wafer-on-Substrate) silicon interposer — the same advanced packaging platform used by NVIDIA's Blackwell and upcoming Rubin architectures. CoWoS Gen 6, targeting 2026 production, supports eight HBM4 stacks alongside dual compute chiplets on the N3 process node.

The interposer acts as a dense communication bridge enabling memory bandwidth in the terabytes-per-second range, physically impossible with traditional PCB-based connections. TSMC demonstrated direct-to-silicon liquid cooling on CoWoS achieving thermal resistance of 0.055°C/W at 40 ml/s coolant flow — validating sustained operation at 2.6 kW TDP on a 3,300 mm² interposer.

The MiiPU uses a 3,300 mm² interposer housing: 1 CR-Tile (N4), 1 SP-Tile (N4 + analog BEOL), 2 Mii-Tiles (N4), and 4 HBM4 stacks.

### 8.2 Die-to-Die Interconnect (UCIe 3.0)

The UCIe 3.0 specification (released August 5, 2025) delivers 48/64 GT/s speeds for both UCIe-S (2D) and UCIe-A (2.5D) packaging, with runtime recalibration enabling power-efficient link retuning without full reinitialization. Extended 100 mm sideband reach supports the multi-chiplet configuration of the MiiPU.

Three UCIe 3.0 links at 64 GT/s each provide:
- CR-Tile ↔ Mii-Tile Primary: 64 GT/s (1 TB/s effective bandwidth at 128b/130b encoding)
- SP-Tile ↔ Mii-Tile Primary: 64 GT/s (1 TB/s)
- Mii-Tile Primary ↔ Mii-Tile Secondary: 64 GT/s (1 TB/s, for Sheaf parallel execution)

Total intra-package bandwidth: **3 TB/s** across the three UCIe links.

### 8.3 Memory Subsystem (HBM4 × 4)

Four HBM4 stacks (SK Hynix 16-Hi, qualification complete as of late 2025) provide:
- Per-stack bandwidth: 2 TB/s (2,048-bit interface at 8 Gb/s, exceeds JEDEC spec at 10 GT/s)
- Per-stack capacity: 48 GB (16-Hi configuration)
- Total aggregate bandwidth: **8 TB/s**
- Total capacity: **192 GiB**

JEDEC JESD270-4 (April 2025) specifies 32 independent channels per HBM4 stack (double HBM3's 16), providing greater access parallelism for the irregular memory access patterns of the CR-layer translation-table and sheaf-gluing operations.

Memory allocation across the 192 GiB:
- CR proposition store + trace buffer overflow: 32 GiB
- SP embedding store + association graph: 64 GiB
- Translation table overflow (beyond TCAM): 48 GiB
- Sheaf section working set: 32 GiB
- System / OS / compiler workspace: 16 GiB

